    "validation_file": {
      "id": "2",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import re\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\ndef read_lines():\n    with open('main.py') as main_file:\n        return main_file.read().splitlines()\n\n\ndef code_only(line):\n    return line.split('#')[0].rstrip()\n\n\nclass TestWriteIf(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Run the student's code once and save the results\"\"\"\n        cls.log = ValidationProtocol().get_neighborhood_log()\n        cls.painter = cls.log.painter_logs[0] if cls.log.painter_logs else None\n\n    def need_painter(self):\n        if self.painter is None:\n            self.skipTest(\"Your code didn't run far enough to check yet. Make sure the starter code is still there, and check that every if line ends with a colon and every line under it is indented.\")\n\n    def test_if_checks_the_bucket(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program has an if that checks is_on_bucket()\"\"\"\n        ifs = [code_only(l) for l in read_lines() if re.match(r'^\\s*if\\s+.*is_on_bucket\\s*\\(', code_only(l))]\n        if not ifs:\n            self.fail(\"Add an if that checks is_on_bucket() after a move(), like if is_on_bucket():\")\n        if any(not l.endswith(':') for l in ifs):\n            self.fail(\"Every if line needs a colon at the end.\")\n\n    def test_take_paint_is_inside_the_if(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter only takes paint inside an if\"\"\"\n        for line in read_lines():\n            if re.match(r'^take_paint\\s*\\(', code_only(line)):\n                self.fail(\"take_paint() should only happen when the Painter is on the bucket. Indent it under your if is_on_bucket(): line.\")\n\n    def test_walks_the_street(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter walks to the end of the street\"\"\"\n        self.need_painter()\n        end = self.painter.ending_position\n        self.assertEqual(\n            (end.x, end.y),\n            (5, 0),\n            \"Your Painter should end at the end of the street. Keep all five move() lines.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_takes_all_the_paint(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter takes all 3 units of paint from the bucket\"\"\"\n        self.need_painter()\n        self.assertEqual(\n            self.painter.ending_paint_count,\n            3,\n            \"Your Painter should end holding all 3 units of paint from the bucket. Check where each if is, and how many times it takes paint.\"\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "Misconceptions and what to test for:\r\n\r\n- This is the \"write it yourself\" level. Students write the if from scratch, so check the four parts: the if keyword, the condition (is_on_bucket()), the colon, and the indented block. Ask them to point to each part.\r\n- The same question is asked at every square, and only the third check is True, because the bucket is at the third square. Ask: \"Which checks were True, and which were False? Why?\"\r\n- take_paint() takes one unit per call, and the bucket holds 3 units, so the block needs three calls. If a student asks for a shorter way, say yes, and that the lesson on loops answers it.\r\n- A missing colon is a SyntaxError, and a missing indent is an IndentationError (\"expected an indented block\"). Neither runs, so students read the message. Validate also checks the colon.\r\n- Forgetting the parentheses (if is_on_bucket:) asks about the function itself, which is always True, and Validate does not count it as a check. Ask: \"Why does is_on_bucket() have parentheses?\"\r\n- A take_paint() that is not indented runs every time. On squares without a bucket it prints \"There is no paint to collect here.\" and the program keeps going. Validate flags it.\r\n- Placing the if before the move checks the wrong square. The Painter is never on the bucket when the check runs, so it ends with no paint. Ask: \"Where is the Painter when this check runs?\"\r\n- A student can write a single if after the third move and get the right result. Accept it, and ask: \"How did you know where the bucket was? What if you hadn't?\" The checks at every square are what a program does when it doesn't know.\r\n- The street ends at a taxi. The Painter stops one square before it, so the starter's five moves are safe. A sixth move() crashes with \"Painter tried to move off the grid or into an obstacle.\"\r\n- if is_on_bucket() == True: also works. Accept it.\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the program. Ask them to explain which checks were True and why.\r\n\r\nValidation checks that the program has an if that checks is_on_bucket() with a colon, that every take_paint() is indented under an if, that the Painter ends at the end of the street, and that it ends holding all 3 units of paint."
